## 1. Introduction
Enzymes are natural catalysts, which facilitate the biological transformation in living organisms. Using enzyme to manufacture valuable products is an emerging alternative way to the conventional synthetic chemistry approaches, which need harsh reaction conditions, large amounts of solvent consumption and waste generation [1]. With the rapid development of biotechnologies, such as genetic engineering, rational protein design and directed evolution, the applications of enzymes in biosynthesis are also expanding [2]. However, like all the other catalysts, enzymes also suffer from the fragile nature, such as low thermal or pH stability, and bad tolerance to organic solvents [3]. To overcome these drawbacks, a potential solution is to fix the enzyme in a distinct support or matrix for recycling. This kind of strategy is also called "enzyme immobilization."
Enzyme immobilization in nanoscale Metal-Organic Frameworks (n-MOFs) has attracted extensive attention in biocatalysis [4]. The porous structures of MOFs inherently enable enzymes to be encapsulated within their cages [5]. However, since some enzymes are larger than the cavity in size, this strategy may be helpless at certain time. Except for the strategy of encapsulation, another one strategy of hanging enzyme on the surface of MOFs through the formation of bonding and nonbonding interactions has also been exploited [6]. Among them, covalent bonding linkage is more stable, and possesses the advantage of high loading capacity as well. In general, the surface of MOFs could be modified with some well-designed groups to react with the function groups of enzymes such as amino group, sulfydryl, et al. By this way, MOFs such as MIL88B-NH\(_2\), MIL-101-NH\(_2\) and UiO-66-NH\(_2\) were reported to immobilize lipase [7], laccase [8] and Soybean epoxide Hydrolase [9], respectively, and demonstrated extensive potential in biological catalysis.
D-Alanine (D-Ala) is a nonessential amino acid in human body and has several physiological activities including energy metabolism, antibacterial and so on [10]. It also serves as an important industrial chemical, so its industrial enzymatic synthesis deserves deep study. Bacterial D-amino acid transaminase catalyzes the synthesis of D-alanine for the bacterial cell wall peptidoglycan [11] and it inspired us that the enzyme may works in vitro as well. Hence, we cloned the DAT gene from Bacillus
sp. (strain YM-1) and insert it into E. coli BL21 (DE3) for the recombined expression. As we know, the application of purified enzyme in industry needs an initial complex purified process which should be avoided if possible. Therefore, in this study, we attempted to seek a suitable carrier for immobilizing crude DAT.
Among Zirconium-based MOFs, UiO-66-NH\(_2\) is the aminofunctionalized form of UiO-66 with practical thermal and chemical stability, which makes it suitable for enzyme immobilization [12]. In this study, the amino groups of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) were post-synthetically modified by the aldehyde groups in glutaraldehyde and formed Schiff's base (Scheme 1). DAT was covalently crosslinked on the surface of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) through the short chain. The catalysis performance of hybrid MOF-enzyme composite was investigated, which not only maintained high activity but also got rid of the interference of polluting enzymes.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
All chemicals and reagents for synthesizing UiO-66-NH\(_2\) were at least AR grade. 2-Aminoterephthalic acid (BDC-NH\(_2\), purity \(\geq99.9\%\)), zirconium chloride (ZrCl4, purity \(\geq99.9\%\)) and solvent DMF were purchased from Aladdin (Shanghai, China).
Bifunctional reagent glutaraldehyde (GA) was purchased from Macklin (Shanghai, China), \(25\%\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). D-Aspartic acid (D-Asp, purity \(\geq99\%\)) was provided by Adamas-beta (Shanghai, China). D-Alanine (D-Ala) (purity \(\geq98\%\)), sodium pyruvate (purity \(\geq98\%\)) and pyridoxal \(5^{\prime}\)-phosphate (PLP) (purity \(\geq98\%\)) were purchased from Energy Chemical (Shanghai, China).
## 2.2. Gene cloning and heterogenous expression
The DAT gene from Bacillus sp. (strain YM-1) used in this study was synthesized by TsingKe Biotech Co., Ltd. (Nanjing, China). For better binding to the aldehyde groups of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) surface, to the C-terminal of the target protein was added a KKKKKK (six lysines) short chain, because the \(\varepsilon\)-NH\(_2\) of lysine is usually the primary locus to react with the aldehyde group. Recombined E. coli BL21 (DE3) carrying the plasmid pET-Duet1-DAT was constructed in our laboratory. After gene sequencing verified by TsingKe, the obtained positive clones were inoculated in \(30\,\mathrm{mL}\) of LB medium with \(100\,\mathrm{mg}\) ampicillin/L and induced by IPTG to express the D-alanine aminotransferase (EC: 2.6.1.21). Scale-up fermentation was operated in a \(5\,\mathrm{L}\) fermenter in our laboratory and obtained \(200\,\mathrm{g}\) DAT-engineered cells.
