The successfully expressed cells were acquired by centrifugation at \(10{,}000\times g\) under \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\). Take \(5\,\mathrm{g}\) obtained cell to disperse in \(50\,\mathrm{mL}\) PBS buffer (pH 7.0, \(20\,\mathrm{mM}\)) and then conduct the ultrasound breaking. The intracellular proteins including over-expressed DAT of the engineered cells were released and the crude enzyme solution was kept for immobilization later.
## 2.3. Synthesis of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-GA
\(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) was prepared according to the previous reference [13] with some modification. An optimized mixture was conducted: \(0.12\,\mathrm{g}\) \(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\), \(0.11\,\mathrm{g}\) BDC-NH\(_2\) and \(1.9\,\mathrm{g}\) benzoic acid was dissolved in \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) DMF solution and maintained \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). After being cooled to room temperature, the obtained suspension was centrifuged at \(10000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) and washed with methanol, ethyl alcohol and diluted water 3 times, respectively. The resulting wet powder was dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) to get the white powder UiO-66-NH\(_2\). After repeating this procedure multiple times, we obtained \(3\,\mathrm{g}\) UiO-66-NH\(_2\) solid.
The surface of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) was modified with glutaraldehyde as follows: \(1\,\mathrm{g}\) UiO-66-NH\(_2\) was dispersed evenly in \(30\,\mathrm{mL}\) ethyl alcohol under ultrasound and added \(3\,\mathrm{mL}\) \(25\%\) glutaraldehyde, then the mixture was heated to reflux for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\). After the reaction finished, the mixture turned to yellow from white. The product was centrifuged at \(10000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) and washed three times, dried to obtain light yellow powder UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-GA.
## 2.4. D-amino acid transaminase immobilization
## 2.4.1. General process
The initial protein concentration of crude enzyme solution was determined as \(9.04\,\mathrm{mg/mL}\) by Bicinchoninic acid (BCA) assay [14]. Before UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-GA immobilized the enzyme, the enzyme solution was diluted twice. Then, different weight of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-GA was added to \(2\,\mathrm{mL}\) enzyme solution for binding the target protein. The crosslinking reaction was performed by shaking table under \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(200\,\mathrm{rpm}\).
## 2.4.2. Parameters optimization
To explore the optimal immobilization conditions, the effects of UiO-66-NH2-GA dosage and immobilization time were investigated. To observe the effect of UiO-66-NH2-GA dosage, varying amounts of UiO-66-NH2-GA (60 mg, 80 mg, 100 mg, 120 mg, 140 mg, respectively) was added to the reaction mixture and incubated for \(5\,\mathrm{~h~}\) for immobilizing completely. To observe the effect of reaction time, different reaction time (0.5 h, 1 h, 2 h, 3 h, 4 h, 5 h, 6 h) was investigated.
After the reaction completed, the mixture was centrifuged at 10000 rpm for 5 min to separate the supernatant and precipitation. The residual protein concentration was determined by BCA assay. Herein, protein measurements were used to determine the immobilization yield for preliminary assessment of immobilization effect [8,15]. The immobilization yield was calculated as following equal: A0 and A1 were the initial and residual concentration of protein \(\mathrm{(mg/mL)}\).
\[
\mathrm{Immobilized~yield~}(\%)=\frac{(A1-A0)}{A0}\times100\%
\]
To describe the success of immobilization, activity recovery was defined as the activity of the immobilized enzyme compared with that of the total starting activity of the free enzyme. It was calculated as the following equal: E0 and E1 were the initial enzyme activity of free enzyme and residual enzyme activity of immobilized enzyme \(\mathrm{(U/mL)}\).
\[
\mathrm{Activity~Recovery~}(\%)=\frac{\mathrm{E1}}{\mathrm{E0}}\times100\%
\]
Moreover, the enzyme activity unit \(\mathrm{[1\U/mL)}\) was defined as the DAla produced per hour (mM) in \(2.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) mixture.
## 2.5. Characterization
The morphology of UiO-66-NH2 and UiO-66-NH2-GA-DAT was characterized by Scanning Electron Microscope (S-3400 N, Japan Hitachi). The thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA, Pyris 1 DSC, PerKinElmer) recorded the mass variation under temperature ranging from 30 to \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\) All the characterization was tested in Center of Modern Analysis, Nanjing University.
## 2.6. D-Ala synthesis
The D-Ala synthesis experiments were performed in a \(4\,\mathrm{\mL}\) Eppendorf tube. Initially, 1 mL 100 mM D-Asp solution mixing with \(400\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\,0.5\%\) Pyridoxal Phosphate (PLP) solution and \(2\;\mathrm{mg}\) pyruvic acid was added to the tube. Then, add \(110\,\mathrm{\mg\UiO{-}66{-}N H_{2}}\) -GA-DAT or \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) undiluted enzyme solution for catalyzing. The final reaction volume was set as \(2.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) by filling with PBS buffer. HPLC was used for detecting the concentration of D-Asp and D-Ala in the mixture.
## 2.7. Thermal stability and recyclability
Many reported MOFs enzyme composites exhibited catalytic superiority than free enzymes, especially in stability and recyclability [16–18]. Herein, we examined the thermal stability and recycle using times of UiO-66-NH2-GA-DAT and free enzyme.
Firstly, UiO-66-NH2-GA-DAT and free enzyme were kept in different temperature \((30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) for biosynthesizing D-Ala. After the first-time reaction was over, we centrifuged the reaction solution in \(40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) water bath and disposed of the supernatant. The left immobilized enzyme was reused to react.
## 2.8. Scale up of reaction
Given the success achieved in \(2.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) reaction, we scaled up the reaction volume to \(200\;\mathrm{mL}\) . Remaining the substrate (D-Asp, \(40\,\mathrm{mM}\) ) concentration unchanged, we added \(8\,\mathrm{~g~}\) UiO-66-NH2-GA-DAT in the
Given the success achieved in \(2.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) reaction, we scaled up the reaction volume to \(200\;\mathrm{mL}\). Remaining the substrate (D-Asp, \(40\;\mathrm{mM}\)) concentration unchanged, we added \(8\;\mathrm{g}\) UiO-66-NH2-GA-DAT in the reaction mixture, other conditions were also changed with proportion. The ninhydrin colorimetry assay was used to monitor the reaction process. After the reaction completed, the mixture was centrifuged first to isolate immobilized enzyme, and then we concentrated the reaction solution by rotary evaporators. After adjusting the pH to 3, the unreacted Asp crystal precipitated. After filtering to remove D-Asp, we adjusted the pH to 1. The D-Ala was obtained by cationic column chromatography isolation, vacuum concentration and crystallized by adding ethyl alcohol
## 2.9. Analytical methods
The amino acids in the mixture were pre-column derived by Marfey's Reagent (1-fluoro-2,4-dihitrophenyl-5-L-alanine amide), generating a characteristic absorption in \(340\;\mathrm{nm}\) [19]. With a C18 \((250\times4.6\;\mathrm{mm},\,5\;\upmu\mathrm{m})\) column fitted in the HPLC machine (LC-20A, SHIMADZU), we used MeCN-0.1% TFA aqueous as the mobile phase. Elution program was set in a linear gradient of MeCN from 20 to \(45\%\) in \(45\;\mathrm{min}\) at a flow rate of \(0.8\;\mathrm{mL/min}\) [20]. Analysis of standard substances was performed primarily to determine the retention time of each ingredient.
Moreover, ninhydrin ethanolic solution was also used as chromogenic agent in acid condition to quantitative detection D-Ala, and the absorption wavelength of obtained product was detected at \(568\;\mathrm{nm}\) by the absorption spectrophotometer.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Design principle of UiO-66-NH2-GA-DAT
D-amino acids are chiral isomer of L-amino acids which are not incorporated into proteins synthesis but more biologically potent. Our laboratory has worked on industrial enzymatic synthesis of D-amino acids for more than ten years [21,22]. D-Ala is an important industrial chemical with great value. We attempted to construct an in vitro biosynthesis reaction to synthesis D-Ala by crude D-amino acid transaminase. For getting more cycle times of DAT enzyme, we adopted a stable Metal-Organic Frameworks UiO-66-NH2 to immobilize it. Our original purpose was to obtain hybrid enzyme with stable activity and easiness to recycle. However, during the experiments, we found the obtained enzyme not only maintained high activity but also got rid of the interfering enzymes, such as Alanine racemase (Alr) and other racemase expressed by the genome of Escherichia coli [23,24]. As we all know, purifying the recombinant protein through Ni-chelating affinity chromatography is a complex process which consuming a major expenditure of time and effort. However, if we skip this step, the substance D-Asp and produced D-Ala in the reaction process will be racemized by polluting enzymes. In this study, after immobilized onto UiO-66-NH2, the racemization was suppressed and the L-isomers were almost undetectable. These results might give some hints for further development of enzyme immobilization.
## 3.2. Protein size measurement
The DAT gene from Bacillus sp. (strain YM-1) was chosen as the research object because our previous study indicated that this enzyme had superior activity (Data not published yet). Before we immobilized this enzyme onto the UiO-66 surface, a question was: the protein and UiO-66-NH2 particle, which one is more bulky? With this question in mind, we downloaded the PDB file of DAT structure from PDB database (ID: 1DAA) [25]. The crystallographic structure of DAT was showed as a dimer containing two identical subunits A and B. As shown in Fig. 1a, the distance between Glu273 (Chain B) and Thr 274 (Chain A) was 72.660 Å, and the distance between His 228 (Chain B) and Lys 11 (Chain B) was 57.713 Å. Fig. 1b showed that the distance between Asp
49 (Chain B) and Glu 161 (Chain B) was about 39.096 Å. From these distance data we could roughly estimated that \( D A T \) is a dimer with volume about \( 7.26\,\mathrm{nm}\times5.77\,\mathrm{nm}\times3.90\,\mathrm{nm} \), which is undoubtedly smaller than the UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) particle.
## 3.3. Gene cloning and heterogenous expression
Successfully cloned recombinant cell was inoculated in \( 3~\mathrm{mL} \) of LB medium supplemented with \( 100\,\mathrm{mg} \) ampicillin/L. After the medium becoming turbid, \( 5\% \) seed culture was inoculated into \( 30\,\mathrm{mL} \) LB medium in \( 100\;\mathrm{mL} \) flask. Upon the optical density at \( 600\;\mathrm{nm} \) reached 0.5, the expression of \( D A T \) was induced by adding IPTG to a final concentration of \( 0.5\;\mathrm{mM} \), and the culture was incubated at \( 28~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and \( 200~\mathrm{rpm} \) for \( 8\,\mathrm{h} \). By shaking flask fermentation, we only got \( 2\,\mathrm{g} \) cells.
Then, scale-up cell culture and fermentation was conducted in a \( 5\,\mathrm{L} \) fermentation tank (Fig. 2a). Using this mechanized devices, we obtained \( 200\,\mathrm{g} \) wet cells at one trial. Furthermore, the expression of protein was analyzed by SDS-PAGE electrophoresis. The experimental procedure was conducted according to SDS-PAGE Gel Kit purchased from Beijing Solarbio Science & Technology Co., Ltd. As shown in Fig. 2b, a distinct band appeared at about \( 35\,\mathrm{kDa} \), which matched well with the molecular weight of His tag-DAT-KKKKKK. The result from SDS-PAGE indicated that the \( D A T \) protein was expressed excessively, meanwhile, some polluting enzyme also expressed.
## 3.4. Synthesis and characterization of MOF hybrid enzyme
The immobilization of \( D A T \) onto \( \mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2} \) was illustrated in Scheme 1. Glutaraldehyde (GA) was used as the crossing linker because
it has two amino sides which can easily access the lysine of proteins and cross-link them [26]. As shown in Fig. 3a, the optimal dosage of UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) was approximately \( 120\;\mathrm{mg}/9\;\mathrm{mg} \) \( D A T \) protein, which was slightly lower than the previous results reported by Cao et al [9] that Soybean Epoxy Hydrolase (SEH) loading was \( 87.3\;\mathrm{mg/g} \) UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \). Additionally, as shown in Fig. 3b, the optimal cross-linking time was about \( 3\;\mathrm{h} \), during which the system achieved \( 95\% \) immobilization rate. With the time extension \( (4-6\mathrm{~h}) \), the final immobilization rate only rose \( 1\% \), which indicated the saturation of the reaction. Overall, the enzyme activity recovery was ranging from \( 27\%-50\% \).
The powder appearances of UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \), UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-GA and UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-GA-DAT were shown in Fig. 4a–c. The modification of the UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) with GA brought some colour variation from pale white to bright yellow, and the yellow became darker after the immobilization of the protein. The morphologies of the MOFs and immobilized enzyme were characterized via Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM) and Transmission Electron Microscope (TEM) (Fig. 4d–i). The diameter of UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) was about \( 100{-}200\ \mathrm{nm} \). As we claimed above, the volume of \( D A T \) protein was about \( 7.26\ \mathrm{nm}\times\,5.77\ \mathrm{nm}\times\,3.90\ \mathrm{nm} \), which seemed much smaller than the volume of MOFs. Overall, Fig. 4d and g showed that the angular \( \mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2} \) became a regular spherical shape (scale bar: \( 5\;\upmu\mathrm{m} \)). The surface of UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) became blurred because \( D A T \) was attached. It was more clearly from Fig. 4h and i which are magnified to \( 200\,\mathrm{nm} \) and \( 20\,\mathrm{nm} \) scale, respectively.
The MOFs and immobilized enzyme were characterized by powder XRD (Fig. 5a). The XRD pattern of UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \)-GA-DAT was the same as that of UiO-66- \( \mathrm{NH}_{2} \) which were consistent with the previous study [13]. The results indicated that the crystal structure of UiO-66-NH2 was basically kept before and after the immobilization. Thermal
Gravimetric Analysis (TGA) was recorded to monitor weight changes of MOFs and immobilized enzyme as a function of temperature and time. The temperature was recorded from \(30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a heating rate \(5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\). Compared with the curves, a different weight loss tendency was observed from \(30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) may due to the departure of the enzyme molecules from the MOFs surface.
## 3.5. The catalytic performance of immobilized enzyme
Application of DAT free enzyme and UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT to synthesis D-Ala was further studied. D-Asp was chosen as the amino donor for
Application of DAT free enzyme and UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT to synthesis D-Ala was further studied. D-Asp was chosen as the amino donor for transforming pyruvic acid to D-Ala, an important chiral blocks. The catalytic performance of crude enzyme was first investigated. As shown in Fig. 2b before, in the process of over-expression of DAT, some polluting enzyme also expressed. Previous study demonstrated that there were some racemase gene especially Alanine racemase (eg. dadX) in the Escherichia coli's genome. Hence, we assumed that the obtained D-Ala via DAT transamination would be racemized to L-isomer by undesired racemase [27]. Same as what we predicted, from Fig. 6a and Fig. 6b, it was apparent that the concentration of L-isomers gradually increased in the duration of the reaction. In addition, the increase of L-Ala and L-Asp also limited the D-Ala production. To be
specific, the concentration of D-Ala reached the peak value of about \(15.8\;\mathrm{mM}\) at \(1\,\mathrm{h}\).
But after \(1\,\mathrm{h}\), the concentration of D-Ala remained unchanged, the concentration of L-Ala and L-Asp steadily increased. The final yield of D-Ala was about \(37\%\) due to the inhibition of by-product formation. The catalytic performance of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT was also tested. Compared with crude enzyme group, the production of D-Ala at \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) by UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT was only \(7.28\;\mathrm{mM}\), which meant lower relative enzyme activity than crude enzyme in unit time. However, after \(1\,\mathrm{h}\), the concentration of D-Ala in the reaction system increased continuously. In contrast, the concentration of L-isomers was lower than our
detection limit throughout, no matter L-Asp or L-Ala. Finally, the yield reached up to \(76.7\%\) at \(8\,\mathrm{h}\). From the three dimensional Fig. 6D, the whole conversion process could be observed clearly.
## 3.6. Thermal stability and reusability
The thermal stability of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT and free enzyme was investigated by placing the reaction tube into water baths at different temperatures (\(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). The group showing highest activity was denoted as \(100\%\). From Fig. 7a, the relative activity of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT was about \(65\%\) even at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which meant
that the activity loss was about \(35\%\) compared with the group at \(40\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\) However, at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) the free enzyme group decreased to only \(10\%\) of its highest activity.
After the reaction was over, we isolated the solution and UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - GA-DAT. Fresh D-Asp solution was added again to check whether UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT could be used repeatedly. From Fig. 7b, we could find that the immobilized enzyme only lost \(28\%\) after 6 times usage. The reusability of UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT not only reduced the cost but also facilitated the scale-up separation of product and enzyme in industry.
## 3.7. Scale up of reaction
In this work, the volume was scale-up to \(200\;\mathrm{mL}\) . After the \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) reaction, we centrifuged the mixture first. Then we concentrated it to reduce the volume, and adjusted the pH to 3 to obtain D-Asp precipitation. The mixture was then filtered to remove D-Asp, and the pH was adjusted to 1. D-Ala was crystallized after the addition of ethyl alcohol. The final weight of D-Ala was \(0.5\;\mathrm{g},\) , thus the yield was \(70\%\) . \(^1\mathrm{H}\) NMR \({}^{600}\,\mathrm{MHz},\) , Deuterium Oxide) 3.59 (pd, \(J=6.6\) , 5.9, 2.7 Hz, 1H), 1.28 (td, \(J=6.7\) , 6.1, 2.9 Hz, 3H).
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, the main achievements of this study were as follows: (1) an industrial enzyme-DAT was immobilized on the surface of UiO\(66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) for the first time. (2) The immobilized MOF-enzyme UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT was characterized by SEM, TEM and other methods to demonstrate that the enzyme attached the MOF surface successfully. (3) UiO\(66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) -GA-DAT maintained enzyme activity of DAT and acted as an efficient biocatalyst to synthesis D-Ala with a \(76.7\%\) yield. This yield was about twice as much as the yield catalyzed by crude enzyme due to eliminated the polluting enzyme inhibition, especially racemase.
For matching further scale-up in industry, we adopted the crude enzyme in this study rather than pure DAT purified by complex NiSepharose purification. Though almost all the enzymes were immobilized, DAT with modified C-terminal was the leading one. We guessed the reason explaining the phenomenon reported in this paper was: the slight polluting enzymes lost their activity after immobilization while excess DAT was relatively enriched and purified. Moreover, on account of UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -GA-DAT still has some pores that not blocked by enzymes, thus the small-molecule substrate and product may absorbed in it which led to the final detected yield of D-Ala was only \(76.7\%\) . But overall, by immobilization, we obtain D-Ala with a higher yield and optical purity.
